 script with a stub returning a rotated/rejected cursor and asserts it reseeds from the tail, publishes nothing, and reads no full-journal copy.","cohort":["E3","E5","G5","T12","T6","T9","necessity","project.failure-disposition-contract","project.portability"],"criteria":["E5"],"decision":"indeterminate","decision_margin":-0.95,"drop_reason":null,"evidence":["Plan R1: 'Unusable cursor (journald rotated past it, or it is rejected): the same reseed-from-tail, publishing nothing for that run. An unusable cursor never becomes a permanent stall and never silently degrades to a full scan.'","ACs enumerate cold-start-adjacent (AC3), upgrade (AC6), failure/timeout (AC4/AC5) but no AC drives a rotated/rejected cursor through the stub."],"finding":"R1 names four state-transition legs (cold start, upgrade, unusable cursor, failure/timeout) but the 'Unusable cursor' leg — journald rotated past the persisted cursor or the cursor is rejected, requiring reseed-from-tail and publishing nothing that run — has no corresponding acceptance criterion. AC6 covers upgrade, AC4/AC5 cover failure/timeout, but the rotated/rejected-cursor recovery path (explicitly called out as the leg that 'never silently degrades to a full scan') is untested, so a regression that turns a rotated cursor into a full journal scan — the exact defect being fixed — could ship green.","group_criteria":null,"group_id":null,"id":"fd0f6af3ac9b5f9a9","impact":0.0,"is_primary":null,"location":"Repair R1 'Unusable cursor' leg; Acceptance criteria AC4-AC6","norm_id":"n4599b878613db9df","priority":0.0,"reason":"no-verification","recalled":false,"scenarios":["A future edit mishandles a rejected --after-cursor and falls back to a full-journal read; the suite is green because no AC exercises the rotated-cursor leg, reintroducing the original IOPS-saturation hang."],"severity":null,"suggested_fix":"Add AC: 'Given a persisted cursor the journalctl stub reports as unusable (rotated/rejected), the run reseeds from the tail, publishes nothing for that metric, and its recorded read volume is bounded (no full-journal scan).'","tier":"LLM","validity":0.0,"verification":null},{"block_threshold":0.75,"blocking_enabled":true,"checklist_item":"- [ ] Correct the contract citation in 'Why it is a defect': the publish-nothing/absence-means-dead contract is at observability.sh:483-490 (and :487), not :280 (which is the dimensionless-keying note).","cohort":["E4"],"criteria":["E4"],"decision":"indeterminate","decision_margin":-0.75,"drop_reason":null,"evidence":["Plan: \"the probe's contract at `:280` / `:487` is that a probe which cannot measure publishes NOTHING, and that its ABSENCE means the host is dead\"","observability.sh:279-280 reads 'DIMENSIONLESS on both sides ... CloudWatch keys a metric by namespace+name+dimensions, so a dimension on only one side silently never matches' — not the publish-nothing contract","observability.sh:483-490 carries the heartbeat / absence-means-dead / pessimistic-on-failed-read semantics the plan attributes"],"finding":"The 'Why it is a defect' section cites the probe's publish-nothing contract at `:280` / `:487`, but line :280 states the dimensionless-CloudWatch-keying convention, not the publish-nothing contract; the 'a probe which cannot measure publishes NOTHING / its ABSENCE means the host is dead' semantics are actually at :483-490. The substance is backed (at :487), so the claim is verified, but the :280 line pointer is misattributed.","group_criteria":null,"group_id":null,"id":"fc65e244ee533e0ca","impact":0.0,"is_primary":null,"location":"Plan section 'Why it is a defect' — cite `observability.sh:288-292` ... `:280` / `:487`","norm_id":"n2418b27cd3a92811","priority":0.0,"reason":"no-verification","recalled":false,"scenarios":["A reviewer or implementer follows the :280 pointer to confirm the contract and finds an unrelated dimensionality note, undermining confidence in the defect framing"],"severity":null,"suggested_fix":"Replace the `:280` pointer with `:483-490` (or keep :487 alone) for the publish-nothing / absence-means-dead contract.","tier":"LLM","validity":0.0,"verification":null},{"block_threshold":0.6,"blocking_enabled":true,"checklist_item":"Add the steady-state leg (valid cursor, zero new entries) to R1's state transitions: retain cursor and total byte-unchanged, publish 0, and distinguish it from the reseed-from-tail 'unusable cursor' leg.","cohort":["G6"],"criteria":["G6"],"decision":"indeterminate","decision_margin":-0.6,"drop_reason":null,"evidence":["Plan: 'the scan reads only entries after that cursor (--after-cursor with --show-cursor) and adds the new matches to the total'","Plan enumerates cold-start / upgrade / unusable-cursor / failure legs but not the zero-new-entry steady state","On empty --after-cursor output, --show-cursor produces no cursor line, so 'seed/advance the cursor' has no source — the prior cursor must simply be retained"],"finding":"R1 enumerates 'all four legs' of the state machine (cold start, upgrade, unusable cursor, failure/timeout) but omits the common steady-state case: a valid cursor with zero new entries after it. journalctl --after-cursor with no new entries produces empty output, and --show-cursor emits no cursor line — so the plan should state that in this case both the persisted cursor and total are retained byte-unchanged and 0 is published, distinguishing it from the 'unusable cursor' reseed leg.","group_criteria":null,"group_id":null,"id":"f4c19627003f44458","impact":0.0,"is_primary":null,"location":"Repair R1 — state transitions ('all four legs named')","norm_id":"n99b7adb9e6d6f70f","priority":0.0,"reason":"no-verification","recalled":false,"scenarios":["A steady-state tick where no new marker has been emitted since the last run (the overwhelmingly common case)"],"severity":null,"suggested_fix":"","tier":"LLM","validity":0.0,"verification":null},{"block_threshold":0.6,"blocking_enabled":true,"checklist_item":"Specify in R1 how each scan separates the `--show-cursor` line from the counted body of a single `-o cat` stream (e.g. capture full output, extract the trailing cursor line, then grep -c the remainder), and confirm the g2p non-record-anchored pattern cannot match the cursor metadata line.","cohort":["G6"],"criteria":["G6"],"decision":"indeterminate","decision_margin":-0.6,"drop_reason":null,"evidence":["observability.sh:976 g2p counter is NOT record-anchored ('free-form phrase/level matches') and uses grep -ciE over the whole -o cat stream","Plan pairs `--after-cursor` and `--show-cursor` but the count and the cursor are two distinct outputs that must be teed apart from one `-o cat` pipe","No existing code uses --after-cursor/--show-cursor (search returned no matches), so this is net-new plumbing"],"finding":"R1 has each scan both count matches and read the persisted cursor from journald's output, but does not specify how a single `-o cat` stream separates the trailing `--show-cursor` line (e.g. `-- cursor: s=...`) from the body counted by `grep -cE`. Counting and cursor-extraction from one pipe need an explicit split, or the cursor line risks being counted (it will not match the record-anchored `^<TOKEN> \\{` patterns, but a NOT-record-anchored counter like g2p's phrase match at :976 could conceivably match cursor metadata) or lost.","group_criteria":null,"group_id":null,"id":"fc45bcd3407b7ebd6","impact":0.0,"is_primary":null,"location":"Repair R1 — 'the scan reads only entries after that cursor (`--after-cursor` with `--show-cursor` ...) and adds the new matches to the total'","norm_id":"na77685cb804827d3","priority":0.0,"reason":"no-verification","recalled":false,"scenarios":["Any counter's scan, especially the phrase-matched g2p counter, where the emitted cursor line shares the stream that grep -c consumes"],"severity":null,"suggested_fix":"","tier":"LLM","validity":0.0,"verification":null},{"block_threshold":0.95,"blocking_enabled":false,"checklist_item":"Specify R1's on-disk state representation and assert (add an AC) that the pre-R1 reader degrades to reseed/publish-0 rather than fabricating a delta when it encounters a new-format cursor+total file, so a rollback of R1 (or a divergent S3 format) cannot produce the plausible-wrong-number the plan rejects.","cohort":["T11"],"criteria":["T11"],"decision":"indeterminate","decision_margin":-0.95,"drop_reason":null,"evidence":["R1: 'Each counter persists a journald cursor beside its total' and 'Cursor and total are persisted TOGETHER in one state file written temp-file-then-rename'","The current reader at observability.sh:885-888 does `prev=$(cat \"$offset_file\")` then `case \"$prev\" in '' | *[!0-9]* ) prev=$total`, i.e. it reseeds only if the file is empty or non-numeric","Constraints: 'Epic 6202 story S3 is concurrently editing observability.sh' and 'Terraform may be written and validated, never applied' — rollback/revert of R1 while a new-format file exists on disk is a live scenario","ABSENCE: no AC asserts that a new-format file is safely interpreted (or ignored/reseeded) by the pre-R1 reader; AC6 only covers old-format-read-by-new-script, not new-format-read-by-old-script"],"finding":"R1 changes the on-disk state-file representation (bare total -> cursor+total pair) but the plan does not specify that representation, nor assert that the pre-R1 reader survives reading a NEW-format file after a rollback of R1. The forward (old->new) upgrade leg is thorough, but the reverse direction is unaddressed even though the constraints explicitly contemplate rollback exposure (Terraform validated-not-applied; epic 6202 S3 concurrently editing the same offset mechanism in the same file).","group_criteria":null,"group_id":null,"id":"f157caff702e8908c","impact":0.0,"is_primary":null,"location":"Repair R1 — 'Cursor and total are persisted TOGETHER in one state file'; state-transition 'Upgrade' leg; AC5/AC6","norm_id":"n1993b60dd88ebf6e","priority":0.0,"reason":"no-verification","recalled":false,"scenarios":["R1 is reverted/rolled back (or S3's concurrent edit lands a different offset format) after new-format state files have been written; the old reader `cat`s a multi-token cursor+total file","If the new format keeps the total on its own numeric line the old reader may still misparse the whole file as non-numeric and reseed (publish 0) — acceptable — but if it puts the total first the old `cat`+arithmetic could yield a bogus `total-prev` = the plausible-wrong-number the plan forbids"],"severity":null,"suggested_fix":"Choose a state layout that the old reader treats as non-numeric (e.g. keep the total on a line the old `cat|case *[!0-9]*` check rejects, or use a distinct filename for the new format so old files are never co-interpreted), and add an acceptance criterion covering new-format-file-read-by-pre-R1-script.","tier":"LLM","validity":0.0,"verification":null}],"impact_model_version":"plan-v5","material_fingerprint":"156d7c3295a1d17a","material_parts":{"children":["647ea2107ca5072f",0],"description":["9c69167274d25bc2",7700],"description_boxes":["83b7db61ca197115",0],"file_impact":["3f5a52671e612b53",5],"ticket_id":["bb2628665a684e5d",19]},"metrics":{"cache_read_tokens":636287,"cache_write_tokens":184371,"claim_path":"no-llm/no-network (structural; the fast claim check is a local HMAC verify)","det_ms":5140.8,"input_tokens":842076,"llm_calls":28,"llm_ms":177599.2,"output_tokens":32471,"precheck":{"cited_anchor_warning":false},"total_ms":204219.8,"verify_requests":11},"model":"bedrock:us.anthropic.claude-sonnet-4-6","provider_provenance":{"candidates":["bedrock:us.anthropic.claude-sonnet-4-6","anthropic:claude-sonnet-4-6"],"capabilities":{"native_structured_output":true,"prompt_cache_style":"bedrock","supports_temperature":true,"supports_thinking":true,"web_access":"off"},"endpoint_host":null,"model":"bedrock:us.anthropic.claude-sonnet-4-6","provider":"bedrock","ran_model":"bedrock:us.anthropic.claude-sonnet-4-6","region":"us-east-1","region_source":"REBAR_LLM_BEDROCK_REGION","tier":"first_class"}